@@ -0,0 +1,42 @@
+---
+type: adr
+status: draft
+entities: [NewsletterSubscription]
+related: []
+generated: { by: "claude-fable-5-1/1.0", at: 2026-09-29T17:40:46.037Z }
+---
+
+# Newsletter sign-up lives in its own module
+
+## Context
+
+Marketing wants a newsletter sign-up on the blog, and the same sign-up
+ships with the next product. It has to be liftable into another app as
+is, so the blog must not grow a dependency on it.
+
+## Decision
+
+Everything newsletter-specific (routes, controller, validators, model,
+table definition) lives under `modules/newsletter/` and is registered
+with the application as a module in `src/app.ts`.
+
+- The host app's `guren.arch.ts` forbids any file under `app/` or
+  `routes/` from importing `modules/newsletter/**`, type-only imports
+  included. The zero-config module rules alone would still allow
+  `index.ts` and `db/schema.ts`.
+- The root `db/schema.ts` re-exports the module's schema, which is what
+  `make:module` wires and what drizzle-kit reads to generate migrations.
+  The migration itself lives in the host app's `db/migrations/`.
+- A subscription starts pending, with a 256-bit random token. Confirming
+  is a `GET`, because the link is followed from an email client.
+- Addresses are lowercased before they are stored or looked up.
+- Subscribe and unsubscribe return to the `Referer` page only when it is
+  same-origin, otherwise to `/`: the header is request input.
+
+## Consequences
+
+Moving the module to another app means copying the folder, registering
+the module, re-exporting its schema and generating a migration there.
+The blog cannot reach the subscriber list directly; anything it needs
+later has to go through a route or an event the module exposes.
+Sending the confirmation email is not part of this decision.
